WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.134
Pedal Post's depot has two doors.

2
00:00:05.134 --> 00:00:13.913
Riders who work there every day come in at the front, speaking the house shorthand: PP-7781, collected, two and a half.

3
00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:19.362
Partner drivers use the side door, where a clerk hands them the standard printed form.

4
00:00:19.362 --> 00:00:23.192
Both doors lead to the same sorting room, under the same rules.

5
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.323
Our library needs both doors.

6
00:00:25.323 --> 00:00:27.776
The front door is for Kotlin callers.

7
00:00:27.776 --> 00:00:31.356
The side door is for the support team, who write Java.

8
00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:35.357
Now, a question that's easy to be shy about.

9
00:00:35.357 --> 00:00:37.886
Kotlin and Java both run on the JVM.

10
00:00:37.886 --> 00:00:40.485
They compile to the same class files.

11
00:00:40.485 --> 00:00:43.856
So why can't Java just call my Kotlin, as it is?

12
00:00:44.933 --> 00:00:46.154
Here's the catch.

13
00:00:46.154 --> 00:00:50.176
javac, the Java compiler, never reads our Kotlin source.

14
00:00:50.176 --> 00:00:54.127
It reads the class files kotlinc wrote, very literally.

15
00:00:55.033 --> 00:00:56.081
Let's map that.

16
00:00:56.081 --> 00:01:04.319
The house shorthand is what lives only in Kotlin source: reified functions, default arguments, objects, value classes.

17
00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:06.762
The printed form is the class file.

18
00:01:06.762 --> 00:01:11.231
And the partner driver, who reads only what's printed, is javac.

19
00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:17.358
The lesson's demo has a small Kotlin API and a Java class that calls it.

20
00:01:17.358 --> 00:01:21.566
Here are four ordinary lines a Java developer might write.

21
00:01:21.566 --> 00:01:23.526
From Kotlin, all four work.

22
00:01:23.526 --> 00:01:25.557
From Java, how many compile?

23
00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:30.021
None of them.

24
00:01:30.021 --> 00:01:33.441
Let's read javac's complaints, one at a time.

25
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:37.512
First: no suitable constructor found for PlainBus(String).

26
00:01:37.512 --> 00:01:44.475
The Kotlin class has default arguments, and Java got only two constructors: no arguments, or all of them.

27
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.093
Second: no suitable method found for subscribe.

28
00:01:48.093 --> 00:01:49.754
That's the reified one.

29
00:01:49.754 --> 00:01:55.097
It gets pasted into each Kotlin caller, and javac can't paste Kotlin code.

30
00:01:55.097 --> 00:02:01.089
So kotlinc marks it synthetic, made by the compiler, and javac walks right past it.

31
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:05.309
Third: cannot find symbol, constructor ParcelId(String).

32
00:02:05.309 --> 00:02:08.673
A value class's constructor is private to Java.

33
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.706
Fourth: cannot find symbol, method describe(String).

34
00:02:12.706 --> 00:02:17.901
describe takes a value class, so its name in the class file is mangled.

35
00:02:17.901 --> 00:02:23.171
The weight getter on an event comes out the same way: getWeight-RqKWbHU.

36
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.063
That's a name only a compiler could love.

37
00:02:26.063 --> 00:02:30.155
So the point is: Java sees the printed form, not the shorthand.

38
00:02:30.155 --> 00:02:33.532
If we want Java at our door, we print what it needs.

39
00:02:34.433 --> 00:02:39.038
Each fix asks kotlinc to print one small thing on the form.

40
00:02:39.038 --> 00:02:43.408
JvmOverloads: one Java constructor per default argument.

41
00:02:43.408 --> 00:02:48.949
A second subscribe that takes Java's Class: an overload javac can call.

42
00:02:48.949 --> 00:02:53.320
JvmStatic makes an object's member a real static method.

43
00:02:53.320 --> 00:03:00.109
JvmField gives a plain field, and file JvmName names the class for top-level functions.

44
00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:04.125
With those fixes, the demo's Java side runs.

45
00:03:04.125 --> 00:03:06.794
The Class overload delivers the event.

46
00:03:06.794 --> 00:03:10.237
The KClass version works too, the long way round.

47
00:03:10.237 --> 00:03:14.031
And FriendlyBus, with JvmOverloads, takes just a name.

48
00:03:14.933 --> 00:03:23.235
That leaves the value classes, and here's the easy trap: give Java a way to pass a raw string or a raw long straight in.

49
00:03:23.235 --> 00:03:26.763
The value classes lesson warned about exactly this.

50
00:03:26.763 --> 00:03:32.575
JvmName on a function that takes a value class lets a raw long walk right past init.

51
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:36.485
In our picture, that's a side door with no clerk.

52
00:03:36.485 --> 00:03:38.366
It makes Java happy fast.

53
00:03:38.366 --> 00:03:44.383
It also lets in a parcel numbered 7781, which the front door would never accept.

54
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.991
So we put the clerk back.

55
00:03:46.991 --> 00:03:54.907
A facade, the hotel reception desk from Adapter and Facade, is one small object that gives outsiders a simple way in.

56
00:03:54.907 --> 00:03:59.507
Ours takes a string and a long, and builds the value classes itself.

57
00:03:59.507 --> 00:04:01.199
So every init block runs.

58
00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:07.860
In Kotlin, Events.collected builds a ParcelId and a Weight, and hands back the event.

59
00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:13.484
Java calls it with a string and a number, and reads the grams back as a plain long.

60
00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:16.028
Now Java tries the bad id.

61
00:04:16.028 --> 00:04:21.579
The demo prints: refused from Java: invalid parcel id: '7781'.

62
00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:26.413
The side door is easy, and it still checks everything.

63
00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:29.448
Our picture breaks in one place.

64
00:04:29.448 --> 00:04:33.018
A real side door often has looser rules for strangers.

65
00:04:33.018 --> 00:04:37.380
Ours has no rules of its own: the same rules, on a different form.

66
00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:40.057
And some of the side door came free.

67
00:04:40.057 --> 00:04:47.497
Handler in E shows up in Java as Handler of question mark super E, so one Java handler fits any subscription.

68
00:04:47.497 --> 00:04:51.115
The sealed model lets a Java switch skip the default.

69
00:04:51.115 --> 00:04:56.848
And fun interfaces with void methods mean no Java lambda ever returns Unit.INSTANCE.

70
00:04:57.767 --> 00:04:58.788
One guardrail.

71
00:04:58.788 --> 00:05:02.146
Kotlin 2.4 has an experimental JvmExposeBoxed.

72
00:05:02.146 --> 00:05:09.809
In a quick test, the getter on the event interface stayed mangled, so keep the facade until that changes.

73
00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:16.012
And search your own Kotlin for public reified functions: what does a Java caller see?

74
00:05:16.933 --> 00:05:19.544
So, why can't Java just call my Kotlin?

75
00:05:19.544 --> 00:05:23.225
Because Java reads the printed form, not the shorthand.

76
00:05:23.225 --> 00:05:26.772
Print what it needs: an overload, a static, a facade.

77
00:05:26.772 --> 00:05:29.248
Make the side door easy, never loose.

78
00:05:29.467 --> 00:05:30.249
Quick check.

79
00:05:30.249 --> 00:05:34.159
Registry gets a Kotlin function, weigh, that takes a Weight.

80
00:05:34.159 --> 00:05:35.397
What does Java see?

81
00:05:35.397 --> 00:05:39.243
And how can Java weigh a parcel without skipping the check?

82
00:05:42.433 --> 00:05:44.196
A mangled name it can't call.

83
00:05:44.196 --> 00:05:50.212
Add a facade function that takes a plain long and builds the Weight itself, so its init still runs.
